fuse: skip the capability lookup before a write when the server kills privileges

Before the first write to a regular file, file_remove_privs() asks whether the
file carries security.capability, which on FUSE is a GETXATTR round trip to the
server (on virtiofs here about 60 us, synchronous in the write path). The
answer is never acted on when the server negotiated FUSE_HANDLE_KILLPRIV_V2:
the kernel already marks every unprivileged write with FUSE_WRITE_KILL_SUIDGID
and leaves killing suid, sgid and the capability to the server. The VFS caches
a negative answer in S_NOSEC, so only the first write to each inode pays, but
a tree of 34,500 small files written once pays 34,500 times (about 2 s of a
31 s install).

Mark a regular file's inode S_NOSEC as soon as it is created when the server
handles privileges (inode_has_no_xattr() does so under SB_NOSEC, which such a
connection sets, and only for a file without suid or sgid bits). The VFS still
clears S_NOSEC when a chmod gives the file those bits, and a write to a file
that gained them on the server's side is still stripped by the server.

Signed-off-by: smolvm <noreply@smolvm>
---
diff --git a/fs/fuse/inode.c b/fs/fuse/inode.c
--- a/fs/fuse/inode.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/inode.c
@@ -510,6 +510,14 @@ retry:
 			inode->i_flags |= S_NOCMTIME;
 		inode->i_generation = generation;
 		fuse_init_inode(inode, attr, fc);
+		/*
+		 * With FUSE_HANDLE_KILLPRIV_V2 the server strips suid, sgid
+		 * and security.capability on write itself, so the VFS need
+		 * not ask for the capability before the first write to the
+		 * file (a round trip per file on virtiofs).
+		 */
+		if (fc->handle_killpriv_v2 && S_ISREG(inode->i_mode))
+			inode_has_no_xattr(inode);
 		unlock_new_inode(inode);
 	} else if (fuse_stale_inode(inode, generation, attr)) {
 		/* nodeid was reused, any I/O on the old inode should fail */
